#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ACP proxy between buzz-acp and a runtime adapter (claude-agent-acp, codex-acp, hermes-acp, agy).

1. Negotiates ACP v1: buzz-acp's v2 handshake predates the current ACP v2
   schema, so recent adapters reject it; their v1 path matches buzz-acp.
2. Chat commands from the agent's owner, answered without waking the model:

     @agent /model [<id>|default]    @agent /effort [<level>|default]
     @agent /new    @agent /stop     @agent /status    @agent /help

   The patched buzz-acp names each turn's chat (channel, and thread root for
   thread scope) and the Buzz events it answers in session/prompt
   `_meta.buzzHost` (removed here), so settings and actions apply to that
   chat (Solo chat / DM) or thread. Commands and picks are read only from
   those events: prompt text, which message content can imitate, is not
   trusted for them. /model and /effort are saved in chat-settings.json and
   applied before each of that chat's turns (session/set_config_option). /stop
   cancels only that chat's running turn (session/cancel). /new asks the
   patched buzz-acp to start that chat in a fresh session
   (BUZZ_ACP_FRESH_SESSION_DIR). The agent posts a short confirmation with the
   buzz CLI.

   Buzz's composer picks: the owner's message can carry
   ["model", <this agent>, id] and ["effort", <this agent>, level] tags (the
   owner's patched Buzz offers model and effort for hosted agents). A pick
   runs that turn only, ahead of the chat's /model and /effort; a turn
   without one goes back to those, else to what the session ran before this
   proxy changed it (model-restore.json keeps that across restarts).

3. Attention: while buzz-host has Attention on for the agent, each prompt gets
   Buzz's end-of-turn review note (attention-note.txt in the agent's dir).

4. Workspaces: a chat's folders come from `@agent /workspace <path>...`, else
   from its Canvas's Buzz workspace block (Solo workspace folders), read only
   when the owner or one of their attested agents wrote it (workspace.mjs).
   The patched buzz-acp names each new session's chat in `_meta.buzzHost`
   (removed here). Claude and Codex sessions start in the chat's first folder
   that exists on this server, so its CLAUDE.md, .mcp.json and skills load;
   Claude also gets the other folders and the agent's own workspace as
   additionalDirectories. Every runtime (Hermes too, whose session cwd keys its
   agent identity) gets Buzz's per-turn note naming the folders and, for a
   Canvas workspace, the Canvas. Laptop paths (/home/<user>/x, /Users/<user>/x)
   map to ~/x here when that exists.

5. Memory: the first turn each session has in this process gets Buzz's memory
   guide and the agent's core memory (<core-memory>), as Buzz puts them in a
   local agent's prompt when its process starts. buzz-host reads core from the
   relay (memory.mjs); the agent's mem_* tools come from its buzz MCP server.
   That turn also gets <owner-review-drafts>: how the owner's patched Buzz
   shows `buzz agents draft-create` / `draft-update` as review popups.

6. Session controls for Buzz's composer (control socket, from buzz-host):
   `state` (idle / active / compacting), `new` (as /new), and `compact`,
   which runs the session's own /compact (Claude, Codex) or /compress
   (Hermes) as a turn of this proxy's: its updates don't reach buzz-acp, and
   a prompt buzz-acp sends that session meanwhile waits for it to end.

7. Reply fallback: an agent replies by running `buzz messages send`, so text it
   only writes as its answer never reaches the chat (Haiku does this now and
   then). When a turn someone asked the agent for (an event from a person that
   tags it: a mention, a Solo chat or DM message) ends normally with text after
   its last tool call, and the agent posted nothing in that chat during the
   turn (asked of the relay), this proxy posts that text for it, where its
   reply would go. Stopped and failed turns, wakes and turns nobody addressed
   are left alone, and so is a turn the relay can't be asked about.

8. Computer tools by chat: each new session's `buzz` MCP server gets
   BUZZHOST_CHAT (the chat's key), so the computer tools record which chat used
   them (desktop/computer.mjs) and the owner's in-chat preview of the agent's
   desktop shows only in that chat.

Everything else passes through byte for byte.
"""
import atexit, contextlib, errno, json, os, re, shlex, signal, socket, subprocess, sys, threading, time

CHANNEL_RE = re.compile(r"^Channel: .*\(#([0-9a-f-]{36})\)\s*$", re.M)
